\textbf{\textcolor{red}{FOC for maximization --- HIGH SCHOOL (free max) vs UNIVERSITY (KKT, constrained).}}

\begin{tabular}{p{17.2cm}|p{17.2cm}}
\textbf{\textcolor{red}{HS version: free max (F)}} & \textbf{\textcolor{red}{Uni version: constrained max (P), KKT}} \\
\textit{Problem (F):} $\max_{x \in \mathbb{R}^N} f(x)$. & \textit{Problem (P):} $\max_x f(x)$ \textcolor{red}{\;s.t.\; $g_k(x) \geq 0,\;k=1,\ldots,K$.}  \\
\hline
\begin{keydef} \fcnum{1a.} \textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Necessity ($\Rightarrow$).}} \underline{If} $x^*$ is a \textbf{local max}, \textcolor{darkgreen}{$f$ differentiable}, \underline{then}
$$ \boxed{\;\nabla f(x^*) = 0.\;} $$
\emph{\textcolor{orange}{$\nabla f(x^*) = 0$ is a necessary condition for a local max.}}

\hline
\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Notes.}}
$\bullet$ Without \textcolor{blue}{concavity}, $\nabla f(x^*) = 0$ alone $\Rightarrow$ $x^*$ is only a \emph{stationary point} (min, saddle, or max).
\end{keydef}
&
\begin{keydef} \fcnum{2a.} \textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Necessity ($\Rightarrow$).}} \underline{If} $x^*$ is a \textbf{local max}, \textcolor{darkgreen}{$f, g_k$ differentiable} and \textcolor{red}{CQ} holds at $x^*$ (linear approxiation is correct for the taylor expansion, hence the derivative correcly describes the feasible region, see appendix), \underline{then} \textbf{KKT conditions hold (4)}: (1) \textcolor{red}{$x^*$ is feasible ($g_k(x^*) \geq 0\;\forall k$)} and, (2) $\exists$ nonnegative scalars $\lambda^* = [\lambda_1^*, \ldots, \lambda_K^*]$ such that (3)
$$ \tfrac{\partial \mathcal{L}(x^*, \lambda^*)}{\partial x_n} = \tfrac{\partial f(x^*)}{\partial x_n} + \textcolor{red}{\sum_k \lambda_k^*\, \tfrac{\partial g_k(x^*)}{\partial x_n}} = 0, $$the lagrangian is just the derivative of the function + the constraints. 
plus (4) \textbf{\textcolor{red}{CS}}: \textcolor{red}{$\lambda_k^*\, g_k(x^*) = 0\;\forall k$}, that is $\forall k$ if $g_k(x^*) > 0 \rightarrow  \lambda_k^* = 0$. That is, if a constraint is slack, the scalar associated to it is 0. Related to interpretation of the scalar in the setting: the scalar indicates what is the marginal value of relaxing a constraint. If the constraint does not bind.. it has no marginal value
\emph{\textcolor{orange}{KKT are necessary conditions for a max.}}
\end{keydef}

\\
\hline
\begin{keydef} \fcnum{1b.} \textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Sufficiency ($\Leftarrow$).}} \underline{If} \textcolor{darkgreen}{$f$ differentiable} and \textcolor{blue}{concave} \underline{then} $\nabla f(x^*) = 0$ \emph{iff} $x^*$ solves (F), that is, is a **global max**.
\emph{\textcolor{orange}{Under concavity, FOC is also sufficient.}}
\end{keydef}

&
\begin{keydef} \fcnum{2b.} \textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Sufficiency ($\Leftarrow$).}} \underline{If}  \textcolor{green}{$f, g_k$ differentiable} and \textcolor{blue}{concave}  and \textcolor{red}{$\exists\,\underline{x}$ such that $g_k(\underline{x}) > 0\;\forall k$} (\textcolor{red}{Slater}), \underline{then}  KKT holds \emph{iff} \textcolor{red}{a feasible $x^*$ solves (P)}, that is, is a **global maximum**
\emph{\textcolor{orange}{Under concavity + Slater, KKT is also sufficient}}
\end{keydef}
\\


&
\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Notes.}}
$\bullet$ Nonneg-constrained variant ($x_n \geq 0$), \emph{necessity only}: $\;\tfrac{\partial f(x^*)}{\partial x_n} \leq 0,\;\; = 0\text{ if } x_n^* > 0.$

{{img:0|w=520}}
\\
\end{tabular}




\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Key implication of the sufficiency side.}}
\begin{keydef} \fcnum{3.}
\underline{If} the \emph{sufficient}-side assumptions hold ---  \textcolor{blue}{$f, g_k$ concave} and \textcolor{darkgreen}{differentiable} and \textcolor{red}{Slater} (\textcolor{red}{$\exists\,\underline{x}$ such that $g_k(\underline{x}) > 0\;\forall k$}) --- \underline{then}:
$$ \boxed{\;x^* \text{ satisfies KKT} \;\;\Longrightarrow\;\; x^* \text{ is a feasible (global) maximum of (P).}\;} 
$$ **Very GREAT result!**
\vspace{2em}
\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Practical recipe.}} For problem (P): $\max_{x \in \mathbb{R}^N} f(x)$ s.t.\ $g(x) \geq 0$ (i.e.\ $g_k(x) \geq 0,\;k=1,\ldots,K$):
\begin{enumerate}
\item Write all constraints in \textcolor{red}{canonical form} $g(x) \geq 0$.
\item Write the Lagrangian: $\mathcal{L}(x, \lambda) = f(x) + \lambda \cdot g(x)$.
\item Set $\lambda^* \geq 0$.
\item \textbf{FOCs --- one per component} $x_n$, $n = 1, \ldots, N$:$$ \tfrac{\partial \mathcal{L}(x^*, \lambda^*)}{\partial x_n} \;=\; \tfrac{\partial f(x^*)}{\partial x_n} + \sum_{k=1}^{K} \lambda_k^*\, \tfrac{\partial g_k(x^*)}{\partial x_n} \;=\; 0. $$ Note here we have a system with n + k unknowns! If you solve it you find the $x*$ and $k$ that make the lagrangian equal to 0 .

\item To solve the system you have: 
\begin{enumerate}
\item N focs
\item \textcolor{red}{Complementary slackness (CS): $\lambda_k^*\, g_k(x^*) = 0\;\forall k$.}  Recall that $\lambda$ is the marginal value of relaxing that constraint! and recall also that you need to verify is a constraint is slack, either a priori (Inada, non satiation) or ex post (more complex situations). If slack -> the scalar associated is 0. If binds -> you use the constraint as an equality equation in the system (indeed, in the consumer problem you use the Bc to solve the system by subsittution in the FOCs)

\end{enumerate}
\end{keydef}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{How to read the output.}}
$\bullet$ If \textcolor{darkgreen}{differentiability} , \textcolor{blue}{concavity} + \textcolor{red}{Slater} hold $\Rightarrow$ steps 1--6 give the \emph{full} characterization of the optimum (\textcolor{orange}{necessary \emph{and} sufficient}).
$\bullet$ If only \textcolor{darkgreen}{differentiability} + \textcolor{red}{CQ} hold (no concavity) $\Rightarrow$ steps 1--6 are only \textcolor{orange}{necessary}; the candidate $x^*$ may be a \textbf{local max}, min, or saddle. Must compare candidates or use second-order conditions.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{REPRESENTATIVE AGENT ASSUMPTION --- when can a single agent stand in for the whole population?}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Definition.}}
A \emph{representative agent} is a single hypothetical consumer whose demand reproduces aggregate demand. Formally: there exists a utility function $U^R$ and a wealth $W$ such that
\[ \boxed{\;\sum_h x_i^*(p, w^h; U^h) \;=\; x_i^*(p, W; U^R) \quad \forall\, i.\;} \]
\emph{When this works, macro/aggregative analysis collapses to a single optimization problem --- a huge workhorse simplification.}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{When is the assumption valid?}}
Aggregate demand must depend on \emph{total} wealth only, \textbf{not on its distribution} across agents:
\[ \sum_h x_i^*(p, w^h) \;=\; X_i(p, W), \quad W = \textstyle\sum_h w^h. \]

\textbf{Two canonical families that satisfy Gorman aggregation:}

\bigskip

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{1. Homothetic preferences} --- whatever the wealth, agents all consume the same RATIO.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{2. Quasilinear preferences} --- whatever the endowment, agents all consume the same NON-NUMERAIRE $y$.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{REAL ENDOWMENTS --- when wealth itself depends on prices.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Setup: nominal vs.\ real wealth.}}

*Usually*: $x_i^*(p, w)$, with $w$ exogenous.

\emph{Real-endowment problem}: Their wealth is then \emph{endogenously determined} by the value of what they own: $w(p) \;=\; p \cdot \omega  $. Hence: $ \hat x_i^*(p, \omega) \;=\; x_i^*\bigl(p,\; p \cdot \omega\bigr). $

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Step 1 --- The chain rule: two channels for a price change.}}

When $p_j$ rises, the consumer feels it \emph{twice}, by the chain rule: $$ \boxed{\;\dfrac{\partial \hat x_i^*(p, \omega)}{\partial p_j} \;=\; \underbrace{\dfrac{\partial x_i^*(p, w)}{\partial p_j}\bigg|_{w\text{ fixed}}}_{\text{Marshallian price effect}} \;+\; \underbrace{\dfrac{\partial x_i^*(p, w)}{\partial w} \cdot \dfrac{\partial w}{\partial p_j}}_{\text{endowment-wealth effect}}.\;} $$

The agent gets ``richer'' when the price of a good they own rises --- by exactly $\omega_j$ extra dollars per unit of $p_j$ change.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Step 2 --- The first term IS the standard Slutsky decomposition.}}

The first term $\partial x_i^*/\partial p_j$ at fixed $w$ is the \textbf{Marshallian price effect}. The Slutsky equation splits it:
$$ \boxed{\;\dfrac{\partial x_i^*(p, w)}{\partial p_j}\bigg|_{w} \;=\; \underbrace{\dfrac{\partial x_i^h(p, \bar U)}{\partial p_j}}_{\text{substitution } S_{ij}} \;-\; \underbrace{x_j^*(p, w) \cdot \dfrac{\partial x_i^*}{\partial w}}_{\text{(Marshallian) income effect}}.\;} $$


\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Preamble.}}
$\bullet$ Different points on the same Indifference Curve represent the loci with same utility under different prices, hence, requiring different w to be attained
$\bullet$ "Bad" price systems (or "stupid" utility functions) can be very inefficient — they require a lot of wealth to reach a given indifference curve.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{0) Motivation.}}
Look at the image, look at what happened to the consumption of xa. It goes down for two reasons: a sub effect to xb and an income effect.

