So why is $x_b$ going down? If a sub. effect is present, part of the change is being driven by it. We want to \emph{quantify how much of the observed change comes from a substitution effect and how much from an income effect}.



\textbf{\textcolor{red}{2) The process}} 
The agent has choosen the optimal bundle through the foc condition. If a price goes up, the optimal consumption ratio changes (sub. eff., loses some $x_b$ in favour of $x_a$, mathmetically this is the MRS = prices), and this is a movement on the IC + the BC shifts inward (income eff., loses some $x_b$, mathematically this is in the BC ).  

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{3) isolate the sub effect with an EXPERIMENT:}}  if i give the agent some extra money to stay on the same IC I can observe how much a price change affects the relative consumption ratio, without being confounded by the income effect. 

