\textbf{\textcolor{red}{CONTRACT CURVE --- the subset of PS that's mutually-improving over $\omega$.}}

\emph{Plain English.} The Contract Curve is the set of allocations that are both \textbf{Pareto efficient} AND make \textbf{both agents at least as well off as at their endowment $\omega$}.  These are the allocations a pair of agents would ``sign a contract'' for: no party walks away worse off than autarky ($\omega$), and there's no further mutual gain left to extract.

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{(1) Definition.}}
\[ \boxed{\;\mathrm{CC}(\omega) \;=\; \bigl\{x \in \mathrm{PS} \;:\; U^i(x^i) \,\geq\, U^i(\omega^i) \;\;\forall i\bigr\}.\;} \]
Two ingredients:
$\bullet$ \emph{Efficiency}: $x \in \mathrm{PS}$ (cannot improve everyone).
$\bullet$ \emph{Individual rationality}: each agent weakly prefers $x$ to $\omega$ (voluntary participation).


{{img:0|w=900}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{COMPETITIVE EQUILIBRIUM (CE) --- prices and allocation where markets clear and consumers optimize.}}



\begin{keydef} \fcnum{4a.}
\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Definition.}} A pair $(\hat p, \hat x)$ with $\hat p \in \mathbb{R}^N_+$ and $\hat x = (\hat x^1, \ldots, \hat x^I)$ such that:

\textbf{1) Consumer optimization:} for every agent $i$,
$$ \boxed{\;\hat x^i \;\in\; \arg\max_{x^i \geq 0} U^i(x^i) \;\;\text{s.t.}\;\; \hat p \cdot x^i \leq \hat p \cdot \omega^i.\;} 
$$
Recall choice variables vs constraints
\textbf{2) Market clearing:} for every good $n$,
$$ \boxed{\;\sum_{i=1}^I \hat x_n^i \;=\; \sum_{i=1}^I \omega_n^i \;=\; \omega_n.\;} $$

==\textcolor{orange}{Note the CE must be studied given Omega. }==
\end{keydef}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{PARETO OPTIMUM --- UPF and PS, the two coordinate views.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{(1) UPF --- where it lives.}}
$\mathrm{UPF} \subset \mathcal{U}$, where
$$ \mathcal{U} \;=\; \bigl\{(u^1, u^2) \;:\; \exists\, x \in \mathcal{F},\; U^i(x^i) \geq u^i \;\forall i\bigr\} $$
is the set of \emph{utility profiles dominated by some feasible allocation}.

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{(2) UPF --- definition (no domination).}}
$(\bar u^1, \bar u^2) \in \mathcal{U}$ is on the UPF iff
$$ \boxed{\;\nexists\, (\tilde u^1, \tilde u^2) \in \mathcal{U} : \tilde u^i \geq \bar u^i \;\forall i,\; > \text{some } j.\;} $$
set of utiliteis that are undominated


{{img:0|w=580}}

do not care about the line rn pls
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{(3) PS --- definition (no domination).}}
$\bar x \in \mathcal{F}$ is Pareto Optimal iff
$$ \boxed{\;\nexists\, y \in \mathcal{F} : U^i(y^i) \geq U^i(\bar x^i) \;\forall i,\; > \text{some } j.\;} $$
and $\mathrm{PS} = \{x \in \mathcal{F} : x \text{ is PO}\}$. Basically is an allocaiton taht cannot e dominated.

\textcolor{orange}{\emph{The allocation-space picture (Edgeworth box) lives in its own snippet --- ``PS in the Edgeworth box''.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{(4) Finding PS $\equiv$ finding UPF.}}
The two problems are equivalent via the utility map: every $x^* \in \mathrm{PS}$ pushes forward to a UPF point, and every UPF point is the image of some $x^* \in \mathrm{PS}$. Formally:
$$ \boxed{\;\mathrm{PS} \;\subset\; \mathcal{F} \;\;\xrightarrow{\;(U^1,\, U^2)\;}\;\; \mathrm{UPF} \;\subset\; \mathcal{U}.\;} $$

\textcolor{orange}{\emph{Comment: the UPF is the set of all PO allocations viewed in utility coordinates instead of allocation coordinates.}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{PS in the Edgeworth box --- allocation-space view of the UPF.}}

This snippet is the \emph{allocation-space} picture of the very same Pareto-efficient set that appears as the UPF in $(u^1, u^2)$ space. The link is the utility map:
$$ \boxed{\;\text{PS}\;\subset\;\mathcal{F}\;\;\xrightarrow{\;(U^1,\,U^2)\;}\;\;\text{UPF}\;\subset\;\mathcal{U}.\;} $$\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Every gold PO point shown below sits on the UPF}} of the companion snippet --- same set, two coordinate systems.

\textbf{\textcolor{darkgreen}{Picture --- Edgeworth box.}} The green curve is the \textbf{Pareto Set} (PS): the locus of all Pareto-efficient allocations, independent of $\omega$. 

{{img:0|w=900}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Reading the link to the UPF.}}
$\bullet$ \emph{Each gold PO point} in this Edgeworth box is sent by $(U^1, U^2)$ \emph{Each gold PO point} in this Edgeworth box is sent by $\bullet$ \emph{The whole green PS curve} maps onto the whole UPF curve --- nothing is missed.
$\bullet$ \emph{The supporting price slope} $-p_1/p_2$ here corresponds to the isowelfare slope $-\lambda/(1-\lambda)$ on the UPF.

\textcolor{orange}{\emph{Important: Contract Curve vs Pareto Set.} The Contract Curve is \emph{not} the green curve --- it is the subset of PS that Pareto-dominates a given endowment $\omega$ (the agents agree to trade because both end up at least as well off as at $\omega$). PS is endowment-free; the Contract Curve depends on $\omega$ and is only a piece of PS.}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Cross-references.}}
$\bullet$ \emph{Pareto Optimum (PS \& UPF)} --- definitions, the duality box, two planner characterizations.
$\bullet$ \emph{Contract Curve} --- the endowment-dependent subset of PS.
$\bullet$ \emph{Exchange Economy} --- defines $\mathcal{F}$.

