\emph{Reading the Edgeworth picture.} Endowment $\omega = (8, 2)$ for agent 1, $(2, 6)$ for agent 2. Budget line through $\omega$ with slope $-\hat p_a/\hat p_b$. The CE allocation is the unique point where \emph{both} agents' ICs are tangent to the budget line (MRS$^1$ = MRS$^2$ = $\hat p_a/\hat p_b$) --- hence on the contract curve.


\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Four observations on the Edgeworth picture (notes IMG\_1659).}}

\textbf{1) Where the indifference curves sit at $\omega$.}
At the endowment $\omega$, agent 1's IC and agent 2's IC pass through the same point but generally are \emph{not} tangent there. The CE is where, after the rotation of the BC, both ICs become tangent to the BC line --- that tangency point is on the contract curve.

\textbf{2) The BC line is just the consumer budget constraint rewritten.}
Solve the consumer's budget identity for $x^1_\ell$:
\[ \boxed{\;x^1_\ell \;=\; \omega^1_\ell \;+\; \omega^1_h\,\frac{p_h}{p_\ell} \;-\; \frac{p_h}{p_\ell}\,x^1_h.\;} \]
A change in the price ratio $p_h/p_\ell$ \emph{rotates} the BC about $\omega$: the slope of the BC line changes but it still passes through $\omega$. Different rotations $=$ different sets of \emph{feasible} (affordable) bundles for the agent.

\textbf{3) Edges of the box $\Leftrightarrow$ corner solutions.}
An agent ends up on the edge of the box exactly when he sells his \emph{full endowment} of one good. At that allocation:
\[ x^i_n = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \text{the marginal utility of good } n \text{ for agent } i \text{ is } 0\; (\text{at the corner)}. \]
(With strictly monotone $+$ strictly concave preferences, this won't happen in equilibrium; agents stay in the interior. But it's a real possibility once any of these assumptions break.)

\textbf{4) Feasibility}
$\bullet$ \emph{Feasibility}: all points in the Edgeworth box are feasible (no resource is wasted; total endowment is conserved). This is because the axes represent total resources at most. 

